wifi: ath11k: power down the WCN6855 for deep suspend on gts9wifi

The port's WoW quirk keeps the endpoint powered across suspend, which is
fine for s2idle but wrong for deep suspend: the PCIe power domains go
down underneath the live WoW firmware, leaving the chip beacon-tracking
(~800 mW) and occasionally returning with a dead MHI.  Use the normal
power-down path when the target is PM_SUSPEND_MEM; s2idle keeps WoW.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <linux/remoteproc.h>
 #include <linux/firmware.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>

 #include "core.h"
 #include "dp_tx.h"
@@ -1251,6 +1252,22 @@
 {
 	int ret;

+	/*
+	 * The gts9wifi WoW quirk exists to keep s2idle resume fast.  In a
+	 * deep suspend the PCIe power domains are removed underneath the
+	 * live WoW firmware: the chip stays powered and beacon-tracking
+	 * (roughly 800 mW) and the resume can come back with a dead MHI.
+	 * Use the normal power-down path for deep suspend, which lets the
+	 * PCIe/pwrseq stack cut the WCN6855 rails; resume then cold-probes
+	 * the endpoint.  s2idle keeps WoW.
+	 */
+	if (ab->pm_policy == ATH11K_PM_WOW &&
+	    pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_MEM &&
+	    of_machine_is_compatible("samsung,gts9wifi"))
+		ab->actual_pm_policy = ATH11K_PM_DEFAULT;
+	else
+		ab->actual_pm_policy = ab->pm_policy;
+
 	ret = ath11k_core_continue_suspend_resume(ab);
 	if (ret <= 0)
 		return ret;
